# Chuggernaut production config — copy to deploy/prod/chuggernaut.env on the Mini
# and fill in. That copy is gitignored. Sourced (set -a) by boot.sh and the
# run-*.sh wrappers, so `${VAR}` references to earlier vars DO expand.
#
# Repo-relative paths (CHANNEL_BINARY, UI_DIST) are derived by the wrappers from
# the checkout location — do NOT set them here.

# --- State lives OUTSIDE the git checkout so CI checkout / git pull never touch it.
# Set CHUG_DATA to the DEPLOY USER's home (the account the launchd services run as).
CHUG_DATA=/Users/<you>/chuggernaut-data
KEYS_DIR=${CHUG_DATA}/keys
REPOS_ROOT=${CHUG_DATA}/repos
BACKUP_DEST=${CHUG_DATA}/backups
# Served-UI host dir: the native api serves the SPA from here (UI_DIST, set by
# run-api.sh) so web-publish jobs can swap the static front end without a
# redeploy (README §7). update.sh builds web/ and seeds it on every deploy.
UI_ROOT=${CHUG_DATA}/ui

# --- NATS
# The dispatcher/api reach NATS on the host's published port.
NATS_URL=nats://localhost:4222
# The URL injected into agent containers — must be reachable from inside them.
# Single-node (all containers on this machine): host.docker.internal works.
# With a REMOTE worker node it does not (it resolves to the worker itself) —
# use this machine's tailnet IP instead, e.g. nats://100.x.y.z:4222.
NATS_URL_CONTAINER=nats://host.docker.internal:4222

# --- Git front (SSH). Clone URL injected into agent containers; never localhost.
# Same remote-worker rule as NATS_URL_CONTAINER: tailnet IP when a worker exists.
REPO_URL_BASE=ssh://git@host.docker.internal:2222
# Pre-receive hook path as seen INSIDE the ssh container.
HOOK_BIN=/usr/local/bin/chuggernaut

# --- Agent provider defaults (dispatcher refuses to start without PROVIDER).
AGENT_PROVIDER_DEFAULT=claude
AGENT_MODEL_DEFAULT=claude-sonnet-5

# --- Docker fleet.
# Linux with /var/run/docker.sock: leave DOCKER_NODES unset and just set DOCKER_SLOTS.
# macOS + colima: there is NO /var/run/docker.sock, so you MUST set DOCKER_NODES to
# the colima socket (find it via `colima status`), or the dispatcher dies with
# "Socket not found: /var/run/docker.sock". QUOTE the value — the `|` separators are
# shell pipes when this file is sourced. Extra nodes are comma-separated.
#   DOCKER_NODES="local|unix:///Users/<you>/.colima/default/docker.sock|4"
# A `worker` endpoint is a NATS-proxied `chuggernaut worker` daemon on the
# node (spec §3.1) — nothing listens on any network; the daemon dials in.
# Worker-only fleet (README "Worker nodes"): keep the local node registered at
# 0 slots so placement never picks it but failback is a one-line edit:
#   DOCKER_NODES="local|unix:///…/docker.sock|0, nuc|worker|0"
# The slot field means DIFFERENT things per endpoint type (spec §3.1):
#   unix:// or tcp://  — DOCKER_NODES owns that node's capacity, full stop.
#   worker             — membership seed + a PRE-OBSERVATION FALLBACK only. It
#                        applies until the node first reports its own capacity
#                        and can never override one afterwards, so `|worker|0`
#                        is the honest value: the node supplies the real number
#                        on its first ping. This is NOT where you change a
#                        worker's capacity — see docs/reference/runbooks/worker-capacity.md.
# Zeroing an EXISTING deployment's worker seeds is sequenced (that runbook §6):
# the deployed dispatcher must ingest capacity from ping and every daemon must
# be on a build that reports it, or the fleet boots with no capacity at all.
DOCKER_SLOTS=4

# --- Remote worker nodes (optional; README "Worker nodes").
#
# THE RUN SPEC IS DECLARED HERE, NOT INHERITED FROM THE RUNNING DAEMON.
# Everything a `chug-worker` daemon runs with is composed by build-worker.sh
# from THIS FILE at node (re)creation and written to the node's own ENVIRONMENT
# FILE — /etc/chuggernaut/worker.env on Linux, ~/chuggernaut-worker/worker.env
# on macOS — which a systemd unit or a launchd agent hands the daemon (design
# #440 D2). worker-refresh.sh's swap then carries that environment forward
# across self-refreshes. Inheritance is how a value SURVIVES; it is not how a
# value is declared. A setting that is only ever in the daemon's environment
# circulates from one generation to the next and is gone the moment a node is
# recreated without it — silently: caching off (#55), the boot capacity back at
# 4, or a node that keeps serving jobs and quietly stops updating. Declare every
# value a node runs with, here.
#
# PER NODE: any `WORKER_*_<node>` wins over the bare `WORKER_*` for that node
# (the node is CHUG_WORKER_NODE). A fleet's nodes do not share paths — a colima
# node's cache lives under the mac home shared into the VM, a NixOS node's under
# /var/cache — so a single-valued declaration can only ever be true of one of
# them. Prod's shape:
#   WORKER_SLOTS_air=2
#   WORKER_SLOTS_nuc=2
#   WORKER_CACHE_DIR_air=/Users/<you>/chuggernaut-worker/sccache
#   WORKER_CACHE_DIR_nuc=/var/cache/chuggernaut/sccache
# Node names must be shell identifiers to be looked up this way; build-worker.sh
# says so out loud when one is not.
#
# DRIFT: before it replaces a daemon, build-worker.sh compares the node's own
# environment file with the run it is about to compose and REFUSES when the new
# run would drop a setting the live daemon carries — declare it, or say
# `WORKER_SPEC_DROP_OK=1` to remove it on purpose. A node still running the
# CONTAINER daemon has no such file yet, so the live container's environment is
# read instead: the conversion is exactly the recreate this guard exists for. On
# the no-ssh path nothing from here can reach the node, so each refresh instead
# REPORTS the node's own spec into the deploy's task output; compare that
# against this file.
#
# NODE LAYOUT: build-worker.sh installs the daemon binary at
# /usr/local/bin/chuggernaut and the channel binary and refresh script under
# /usr/local/lib/chuggernaut/ (the paths the daemon's own config already
# defaults to), extracted from the worker image it just built. Three knobs move
# the rest, all per-node like everything above and all with defaults that need
# no declaration: WORKER_KEYS_DIR (where worker.creds and worker_git live on the
# node), WORKER_ENV_FILE (the environment
# file's path) and, on Linux, WORKER_UNIT_DIR (default /etc/systemd/system —
# point it at a writable unit path on a node whose /etc/systemd/system is a
# read-only symlink into the nix store). WORKER_PATH overrides the PATH the unit
# or agent gives the daemon, which is also the PATH a host task inherits.
#
# WORKER_KEYS_DIR defaults to /etc/chuggernaut/keys on LINUX, which must be
# owned by root at mode 0700 — the unit runs as root, and the login user this
# deploy ssh's in as is in the `docker` group, so a credential under that user's
# home is readable by anything that user runs (design #440 D5). build-worker.sh
# REFUSES, before it builds anything and with the live daemon untouched, when
# the directory is missing, wrongly owned, wrongly moded, or holds no
# worker.creds — each with its own remedy, README §6. On macOS the daemon is a
# GUI-domain agent running as the login user, so that boundary does not port and
# the default stays ~/chuggernaut-worker/keys at 0600 per file.
#WORKER_KEYS_DIR_nuc=/etc/chuggernaut/keys
#WORKER_UNIT_DIR_nuc=/etc/systemd/system
#
# SSH destination of the node; update.sh/build-worker.sh build the worker
# daemon + agent images there (context streamed over ssh) and (re)start the
# daemon. Leave unset on single-node deploys — and on prod, where it is unset
# for BOTH nodes: Tailscale SSH blocks tagged->tagged, so the Mini cannot reach
# either of them and build-worker.sh no-ops on every deploy (the daemons refresh
# themselves over the worker RPC instead). It is deliberately NOT resolved per
# node: it is the switch that says THIS machine can reach that node, so it is
# passed on the command line by whoever can — the operator laptop. The
# consequence, stated plainly: nothing scheduled ever re-applies this file to a
# prod node. The declaration is what a human recreates a node from, and the
# refresh's report is how they see what a node is running meanwhile.
#WORKER_SSH=worksalot@gumbo-nuc-0
# NATS URL the worker (and its job containers' MCP) reach THIS machine on —
# the tailnet IP form of NATS_URL_CONTAINER.
#WORKER_NATS_URL=nats://100.x.y.z:4222
# Node name for the daemon; must match its DOCKER_NODES entry. Default: nuc.
#CHUG_WORKER_NODE=nuc
# The node's FIRST-BOOT capacity (spec §3.1) — the value it starts at before any
# operator intent exists, not the way capacity is changed. Changing a node's
# concurrency is a runtime command from the Cluster page (or PUT
# /api/v1/platform/fleet/{node}/capacity): no ssh, no rebuild, no restart. See
# docs/reference/runbooks/worker-capacity.md.
# build-worker.sh writes this into the node's environment file and self-refresh
# carries it forward, so a node whose dispatcher is down still boots at a sane
# number; after a swap the node reports this boot value until the dispatcher
# reconciles the recorded intent back onto it (one scan tick). Unset ⇒ the
# daemon's default of 4. Prod runs air (6cpu/12GiB colima) and nuc (12c/31GB)
# at 2 each — so prod declares it PER NODE, and a node recreated without it
# over-commits until the dispatcher reconciles the recorded intent.
#WORKER_SLOTS_air=2
#WORKER_SLOTS_nuc=2
# The CEILING a capacity command is validated against (`WORKER_SLOTS_MAX`, spec
# §3.1) is a knob on the worker DAEMON's env, and it is the one worker knob with
# NO passthrough: neither build-worker.sh nor worker-refresh.sh forwards it, so
# setting it here does nothing. To lower it, add a `WORKER_SLOTS_MAX='<n>'` line
# to the node's environment file by hand — and re-add it after each deploy, since
# the self-refresh swap drops it. build-worker.sh REFUSES to rebuild a daemon
# that carries one (it would drop it) — pass `WORKER_SPEC_DROP_OK=1` to say the
# drop is intended, then re-add the line by hand. Unset ⇒ the node's own CPU count,
# which is the right default unless the CPU count overstates what it serves (air:
# 6 CPUs, but 2 concurrent Rust builds is what it sustains). Must be at least 1.
# See docs/reference/runbooks/worker-capacity.md §5.
# Node-local build cache (spec §3.1 "Node-local build caching"): a HOST path on
# the worker. Set ⇒ the daemon turns on sccache and bind-mounts this dir into
# every job container it launches (at /cache/sccache), so cargo builds reuse
# compilation across jobs instead of compiling cold every task. Unset ⇒ caching
# off, nothing changes. build-worker.sh writes it into the node's environment
# file (env only — the daemon never reads the cache itself; it binds this host
# path into sibling containers via the docker socket) and self-refresh carries it
# forward on every deploy.
#
# The HOST path must exist for a job container to launch at all: since #379 the
# cache is a typed mount, so the engine REFUSES a missing source rather than
# creating it empty. build-worker.sh provisions it on the node before it starts
# the daemon (`mkdir -p`, then `sudo -n mkdir -p`), which covers every node built
# or rebuilt through a deploy or through chug-install.sh's worker-join; a daemon
# you start BY HAND needs the mkdir by hand (docs/reference/runbooks/adhoc-deploy.md §1c).
#
# Declare it PER NODE: the path is the node's, not the fleet's, and on a colima
# node it must sit under a directory colima shares into the VM (the mac home by
# default) — dockerd runs INSIDE the VM, so a path outside any shared prefix
# binds a VM-local directory that dies with the VM while the mac-side path never
# appears. Read a running node's real value off it (README §6) rather than
# assuming this one.
#WORKER_CACHE_DIR_air=/Users/<you>/chuggernaut-worker/sccache
#WORKER_CACHE_DIR_nuc=/var/cache/chuggernaut/sccache
#
# The DOCKER ENGINE the daemon dials (`WORKER_DOCKER_ENDPOINT`, unix:// or
# tcp:// / http://). Unset ⇒ the daemon's own default, unix:///var/run/docker.sock,
# which was true by construction while the daemon was a container with that
# socket bind-mounted in — and is NOT true of a native daemon on a mac, where
# colima listens at ~/.colima/default/docker.sock (the air, 2026-08-06: every
# launch failed `backend unavailable: Socket not found`). On a DARWIN node
# build-worker.sh DERIVES it from the node's own `docker context inspect`, so an
# ordinary mac declares nothing; this overrides that, per node like everything
# else. Derived or declared, it is a SNAPSHOT written into the node's run spec —
# change the node's docker context afterwards and the daemon keeps dialling the
# old path until the node is re-converted. A socket that is not there refuses
# the deploy; a scheme the daemon cannot parse refuses it too, before a working
# daemon is replaced with one that will not boot.
#WORKER_DOCKER_ENDPOINT_air=unix:///Users/<you>/.colima/default/docker.sock
#
# A DARWIN node's Rust toolchain and build directory (`WORKER_CARGO`,
# `WORKER_BUILD_DIR`) — macOS only, and unset is right on every Linux node.
# design #440 D6 extracts the daemon binary from the worker image, which is a
# LINUX container: on a mac that is an ELF file launchd loops on with `cannot
# execute binary file`, so a mac COMPILES its own daemon (#440's 2026-08-07
# correction). build-worker.sh resolves the node's cargo itself with
# `command -v cargo` over ssh and writes both values into the node's run spec —
# so declare WORKER_CARGO only when that answer is empty while cargo works
# interactively, which is the usual shape for a nix-darwin or rustup toolchain.
# A mac with no reachable cargo is REFUSED before anything is built, and so is
# one whose cargo does not exec (a rustup shim with no default toolchain) or
# whose `rustc` is not in the SAME DIRECTORY: cargo resolves its compiler
# through PATH, so declaring an absolute WORKER_CARGO makes `command -v` pass
# while the compile still fails. That directory is what build-worker.sh
# prepends to the launchd agent's PATH, so the node's own refresh can compile.
# WORKER_BUILD_DIR holds the source tree and the cargo target dir, kept between
# deploys so a rebuild is incremental (a few GB); default
# ~/chuggernaut-worker/build. The node's own self-refresh uses both.
#WORKER_CARGO_air=/etc/profiles/per-user/<you>/bin/cargo
#WORKER_BUILD_DIR_air=/Users/<you>/chuggernaut-worker/build
#
# The runtimes the node OFFERS (design #309 P0, #322 W1): a comma-separated list,
# `container` and/or `host`. Unset ⇒ `container`, which is what every node in the
# fleet runs; `container,host` is the both-modes form. Declared per node like the
# paths above (WORKER_MODES_air wins over a bare WORKER_MODES), and build-worker.sh
# refuses everything the daemon's own parser refuses — an unknown name, an empty
# entry (`container,` has one), a repeat — rather than replacing a working daemon
# with one that will not boot.
#
# SETTING THIS DOES NOT MAKE HOST JOBS RUNNABLE. A job type may declare
# `runtime.mode: host` since #309 P1 (job #478), but nothing on the wire carries
# a node's modes, because design #309 P2 owns NodeCapabilities and has not
# landed, so the dispatcher never sees them and cannot place on them.
# Nothing verifies the claim either: no probe for a toolchain, an interpreter or
# a nix daemon, here or in the daemon. The node declares; nothing checks.
#
# AND `host` IS NOT ADDITIVE — it REPLACES containers on that node. #309 P0 has
# no per-request selector, so a node naming host at all runs EVERY launch as a
# host process and ignores the declared image (the daemon warns at boot that this
# is deliberately a lie that must never leave a prototype node). It also refuses
# to start unless WORKER_SLOTS=1 AND WORKER_SLOTS_MAX=1 — one host task per node,
# because two cannot both own /workspace — and WORKER_SLOTS_MAX is the one knob
# no script forwards (see above), so a host node needs that line added to its
# environment file by hand. `container,host` therefore does NOT mean "both": it
# means host, at capacity 1, for every job the node is given. Prod runs neither
# node this way, and #309 P0 is a prototype that has never run on the fleet.
#
# WHAT IT RUNS IS THE NODE, ONCE THE NODE HAS BEEN CONVERTED. build-worker.sh
# installs a natively supervised daemon (design #440 D1/D2), so a host task is a
# process on the node rather than one inside chug-worker — which is what design
# #309's P0 correction ("The shipped daemon is a container") said P0's proof
# needed. A node that has not been rebuilt through build-worker.sh since is still
# running the container and still gives a "host" task that container's
# filesystem, /workspace and docker socket. Prod's nodes are in that state: the
# Mini cannot ssh either of them, so nothing has re-applied this file to one.
#WORKER_MODES_air=container,host
#
# KVM passthrough, for Android emulator work (design #367; the procedure is
# docs/reference/runbooks/worker-kvm.md). THREE settings. The daemon checks for
# the device in ITS OWN view and REFUSES TO START when WORKER_KVM names one it
# cannot see — and a natively supervised daemon's own view IS the node's, so
# there is no `--device` flag to keep in step with the setting (design #440 §5).
# On a node still running the container daemon there is: build-worker.sh used to
# add it, and worker-refresh.sh's swap still carries it forward from the live
# container. Setting the env on a node that has no /dev/kvm is a node-down, not a
# degraded node: the daemon refuses and the supervisor loops the refusal until
# the node leaves the fleet. Unset ⇒ no passthrough at all; nothing about a
# non-Android node changes.
#
# 1/true/on passes the node's /dev/kvm; an ABSOLUTE PATH names another device
# node; 0/false/off is off (the setting rides, no device is attached, and a
# self-refresh of such a node swaps normally). Surrounding whitespace is trimmed,
# the daemon's own reading. Anything else build-worker.sh refuses, before it
# replaces a working daemon with one that could not boot.
#WORKER_KVM=1
# WHO may use it: `owner/project` entries, comma-separated (the JOB_PROJECT
# shape). FAIL-CLOSED — unset or empty grants NOBODY, so enabling KVM on a node
# and granting it to a project stay two separate acts. A malformed or repeated
# entry is a hard daemon config error, never a grant that silently never matches.
#WORKER_KVM_PROJECTS=acme/beacon
# The node's Android SDK, bind-mounted read-only into an allow-listed launch
# beside the device. Name the operator's ACTIVATION-MAINTAINED STABLE path (the
# `environment.etc` entry in the node's configuration.nix), NEVER a /nix/store
# path: a store path is content-hashed, so a pinned one keeps testing the
# PREVIOUS SDK after a bump and then fails with an unattributable ENOENT once the
# store is collected. The daemon refuses a store hash outright (design #367
# §3.5). Unset ⇒ /var/lib/chuggernaut/android-sdk.
#WORKER_ANDROID_SDK_DIR=/etc/chug/android-sdk
# The node's Flutter SDK, a SECOND INDEPENDENT LEAF mounted read-only at
# /opt/flutter for the same allow-listed launch, with FLUTTER_ROOT pointed at it.
# Complementary, not a replacement: Flutter ships Dart, the gradle wrapper and
# the engine artifacts; only WORKER_ANDROID_SDK_DIR ships adb, emulator and
# platform-tools, so an emulator proof needs both. Same stable-path rule — a
# /nix/store path is refused. Unset ⇒ NO mount and NO FLUTTER_ROOT, and the
# launch is byte-for-byte what it was, so an Android-only node needs nothing
# here. NOT realised and NOT GC-rooted: design #373 P2 owns declared toolchains.
#WORKER_FLUTTER_DIR=/var/lib/chuggernaut/toolchain/flutter
# The node's JDK, a THIRD INDEPENDENT LEAF mounted read-only at /opt/jdk for the
# same allow-listed launch, with JAVA_HOME pointed at it. Gradle — which Flutter
# invokes — is not a nix wrapper and cannot resolve a JDK out of the store the
# way avdmanager does, so without this an APK build dies with "JAVA_HOME is not
# set". Name the DERIVATION ROOT (what nixpkgs designates and what a NixOS
# `programs.java` / GitHub-runner JAVA_HOME already exports), not the nested
# lib/openjdk. Same stable-path rule — a /nix/store path is refused. Unset ⇒ NO
# mount and NO JAVA_HOME, and the launch is byte-for-byte what it was. NOT
# realised and NOT GC-rooted: design #373 P2 owns declared toolchains.
#WORKER_JDK_DIR=/var/lib/chuggernaut/toolchain/jdk
#
# Per-task nix GC roots (spec §3.1 "Node-local nix GC roots", design #373 P1;
# the procedure is docs/reference/runbooks/worker-kvm.md §7). The mounts above hand a task
# store paths that NOTHING roots, so the node's weekly nix-gc can collect a path
# out from under a running task. Set this and the daemon realises the node's
# declared toolchain before an allow-listed launch and holds an indirect GC root
# over it, named by task id, for exactly that task's lifetime — released when the
# container is removed, and reaped later if a worker died holding it.
#
# It names a WORKER-WRITABLE host directory. build-worker.sh provisions it
# (`mkdir -p`, then `sudo -n mkdir -p`) and mounts four paths into chug-worker
# itself: /nix/store read-only, the profiles tree read-only, the daemon socket
# dir READ-WRITE (connecting to a unix socket needs write on the inode), and this
# dir read-write at the SAME path — the nix daemon resolves a root path in its
# own namespace. On a node that attaches a KVM device it mounts a FIFTH: the
# DIRECTORY HOLDING WORKER_ANDROID_SDK_DIR, read-only at the same path. Never
# that path itself — `nix-store --realise` resolves its argument CLIENT-side,
# inside chug-worker, and a bind whose source IS the operator's symlink has it
# resolved away by mount(2), leaving the store path's content at a non-store
# path the client refuses. Hence the shape requirement: the stable path must be
# a DIRECT absolute symlink into the store (a systemd.tmpfiles `L+` line), not a
# NixOS environment.etc entry, which hops through /etc/static. build-worker.sh
# refuses a deploy whose path is any other shape, and the daemon re-derives the
# same property from inside the container at boot.
# The daemon refuses to start when any of them is missing from its
# own view, so a deploy that cannot provision the dir REFUSES rather than leaving
# a node looping on --restart=always. Unset ⇒ no realise, no roots, no mounts.
#WORKER_NIX_GCROOTS_DIR=/var/lib/chuggernaut/gcroots
# The client, and why it is a PROFILES path: chug-worker is long-lived and docker
# resolves a bind source host-side at create, so a client resolved into the store
# is pinned to the generation current at the last swap — which nix.gc's
# --delete-older-than collects, out from under the running daemon. The profiles
# tree is itself a GC root. A store-hash value is refused outright.
# Unset ⇒ /nix/var/nix/profiles/system/sw/bin/nix-store.
#WORKER_NIX_CLIENT=/nix/var/nix/profiles/system/sw/bin/nix-store
# The node's nix daemon socket (0666 on a stock node, so no uid mapping is
# needed). Unset ⇒ /nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/socket.
#WORKER_NIX_DAEMON_SOCKET=/nix/var/nix/daemon-socket/socket
# The store PREFIX — what build-worker.sh mounts read-only, and what the daemon's
# boot check requires the toolchain path to resolve into. Only a node that
# relocated its store (nix's own NIX_STORE_DIR) sets this.
# Unset ⇒ /nix/store.
#WORKER_NIX_STORE_DIR=/nix/store
# The realise BOUND, in seconds. The realise runs before the task exists, so no
# resources.task_timeout covers it and an unbounded one would hang the launch
# path. Breaking it FAILS THE LAUNCH loudly (never a retryable no-capacity: a
# realise that broke the bound is not faster on a retry).
#
# 1..45 ONLY, and both the daemon and build-worker.sh refuse anything else. The
# realise runs INSIDE the `launch` RPC, which the dispatcher abandons after 60s
# (store::worker::OP_TIMEOUT) — a longer bound is never reached: the task fails
# on worker transport, the loud named failure above is never seen, and this node
# goes on to launch a container nobody is waiting for. A toolchain that is
# legitimately slower to substitute than this wants a warming job (design #373
# Decision 5), not a bigger number here. Unset ⇒ 30.
#WORKER_NIX_REALISE_TIMEOUT_SECS=30
# PROJECT-DECLARED toolchains (design #373 P2, the runbook §8). A comma-separated
# owner/project allow-list, matched against a launch's JOB_PROJECT exactly as
# WORKER_KVM_PROJECTS is. Unset or empty ⇒ NOBODY, and this node refuses every
# launch whose job type declares `runtime.env` — loudly, by name, never by
# running it against the image's toolchain instead.
#
# GRANTING IT GRANTS EVALUATION. Flake evaluation is client-side and unsandboxed,
# so a listed project's own flake code runs INSIDE chug-worker, beside
# docker.sock, the NATS creds and the git key. It is tolerable only because such
# a node is SINGLE-TENANT (design #373 Decision 1): the boundary crossed is
# platform-vs-project, never project-vs-project. List one project, read its flake.
#
# And the toolchain must ALREADY BE IN THIS NODE'S STORE: the realise is capped
# at 45s (above) while a cold Flutter/Android closure costs tens of minutes, so an
# unwarmed environment does not run slowly — it does not run. Warm it out of
# band, on the project's clock: a scheduled job declaring the same runtime.env is
# warmed by this same pre-launch realise, and a binary cache in the node's
# nix.conf is the operator's half. Requires WORKER_NIX_GCROOTS_DIR; without it
# the deploy REFUSES rather than grant something the daemon can never act on.
#
# A RELATIVE ref (`nix:.#attr`) additionally needs a GIT KEY SCOPED TO THAT
# PROJECT: it is fetched from inside chug-worker with this node's WORKER_GIT_KEY
# (below), and `chuggernaut admin mint-worker-git-key --project owner/project`
# mints a read-only credential the ssh front authorizes for that ONE repo. The
# node has a single such key — the one its self-refresh uses — so it resolves
# relative refs only for the project that key was minted for; anything else wants
# an ABSOLUTE ref (github:…, a public git+https://…) that needs no credential.
# The mismatch surfaces as a raw ssh permission denial in the realise output, not
# as one of this feature's named refusals (the runbook §8 table).
#WORKER_NIX_PROJECTS=acme/beacon
# The FLAKE client — `nix build`, not `nix-store --realise`, because a project
# declares a flake ref rather than a store path. A profiles path for the same
# reason WORKER_NIX_CLIENT is one; a store-hash value is refused outright.
# Unset ⇒ /nix/var/nix/profiles/system/sw/bin/nix.
#WORKER_NIX_FLAKE_CLIENT=/nix/var/nix/profiles/system/sw/bin/nix
#
# NO-SSH self-refresh (spec §3.1) — the path prod uses, since the Mini cannot
# ssh a tagged worker. On every deploy, update.sh requests each `worker`-endpoint
# node in DOCKER_NODES to rebuild its images at the deployed SHA and swap its
# daemon (job containers survive). The daemon fetches the build context ITSELF
# over the ssh front, so it needs two vars on ITS env: WORKER_REFRESH_GIT_URL
# (ssh://git@<tailnet-ip>:2222/<owner>/<platform-repo>.git) and WORKER_GIT_KEY
# (its ssh key, an absolute path ON THE NODE — a native daemon has no /data/keys
# mount, and build-worker.sh refuses a value still naming one). Set them HERE
# (below) — build-worker.sh writes both into the node's environment file and the
# self-refresh swap carries them forward — or, on a node created some other way,
# add them to that file by hand. A node that
# fails to refresh is a WARNING with its version surfaced, never a deploy failure.
# A node with NEITHER set can't refresh — the deploy now says so LOUDLY
# ("node <name>: refresh SKIPPED — no git credential") instead of no-oping quietly.
#
# ENROLLMENT — mint the node's read-only git credential on the Mini:
#   chuggernaut admin worker-git-key --node <name> --project <owner>/<platform-repo>
# writes worker_git + worker_git-cert.pub (a long-lived read-only cert scoped to
# just that repo). Install both into the node's WORKER_KEYS_DIR at 0600 — on
# Linux that is root's 0700 directory, so `scp` cannot write there and
# `sudo install -o root -g root -m 0600` is the step (README §6) — then set the
# two keys below (WORKER_GIT_KEY already defaults to WORKER_KEYS_DIR's
# worker_git) and re-run build-worker.sh to hand them to the daemon.
#
# Both are part of the run spec, so declare them here even though the daemon
# already carries them: a node recreated without the URL comes up healthy, takes
# jobs, and is SKIPPED by every deploy from then on. build-worker.sh says so
# loudly when it is asked to build a node without one. Per-node forms
# (WORKER_GIT_KEY_air, …) apply if a node keeps its key somewhere else.
#WORKER_REFRESH_GIT_URL=ssh://git@100.x.y.z:2222/acme/chuggernaut.git
#WORKER_GIT_KEY_air=/Users/you/chuggernaut-worker/keys/worker_git

# --- Platform self-repo (deploy-drift reporting).
# `owner/project` of the platform's OWN source repo — the chuggernaut project
# this dispatcher is deployed from (as it is named on this platform, not on the
# GitHub mirror). Each scan tick the dispatcher resolves that project's main tip
# and how many commits the deployed SHA is behind it, which is what the Cluster
# page's drift banner shows ("Prod is N commits behind main").
# UNSET ⇒ the banner reads "Deploy drift unavailable — main tip unknown"; nothing
# else changes and nothing fails. chug-install.sh writes this line for you when
# you import the platform repo (`project-import`), so set it by hand only on a
# deployment whose platform repo was imported some other way.
#SELF_REPO=acme/chuggernaut

# --- API / UI
# The api runs natively under launchd (com.chuggernaut.api / run-api.sh), the
# same `chuggernaut` binary the host builds every deploy; it binds 127.0.0.1:8080
# (Tailscale Serve / cloudflared front it — see README §5). run-api.sh reads
# NATS_URL/KEYS_DIR/UI_ROOT/SESSION_TTL from this file; only SESSION_TTL is
# api-specific.
SESSION_TTL=24h

# --- Workload identity issuer (spec §6.7, design #313).
# The identifier a cloud workload-identity provider is registered with. Read
# today by the api alone, which serves /.well-known/openid-configuration and
# /.well-known/jwks.json from it; design #313 S2's minter already takes the
# issuer as an argument and S4 will have the dispatcher hand it this value, so
# every workload token's `iss` comes from the same setting. chuggernaut.env is
# sourced by run-api.sh and run-dispatcher.sh alike (`set -a`), so setting it
# here already covers both. Must be an
# absolute https identifier with NO trailing slash: a cloud STS compares it
# byte-for-byte, and changing it invalidates every registered provider at once.
# Those two routes are served but NOT exposed — README §5c. Unset ⇒ the default
# below, which is what prod's provider is registered with.
#OIDC_ISSUER=https://chug.kasofsk.xyz

# --- Container image tag built by deploy/prod/build.sh (chuggernaut/ssh:$TAG etc).
CHUG_IMAGE_TAG=prod
# Docker compose network name (project 'chuggernaut' => chuggernaut_default),
# used by backup-r2.sh to run nats-box on the same network.
NATS_NETWORK=chuggernaut_default

# --- Backups → Cloudflare R2
# age recipient (PUBLIC key) the backup tarball is encrypted to. The matching
# private key is kept OFFLINE (password manager), never on this machine.
BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# rclone remote:bucket the encrypted tarball is pushed to.
RCLONE_REMOTE=r2:chuggernaut-backups
# How many encrypted tarballs to keep on local disk before pruning.
BACKUP_LOCAL_KEEP=24
